WEBVTT

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:08.829
This video uses the Human Mapping and Variants demo project, the version with results.

00:00:08.829 --> 00:00:13.477
Choose Help, then Demo Projects, and click Download and Open beside it.

00:00:13.477 --> 00:00:16.422
The video Your first project shows each step.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:22.010
A pileup is reads stacked on the reference sequence they matched.

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:29.607
Open the minimap2 result under Analyses, the program that lined the reads up, select its row, and click Focus.

00:00:29.607 --> 00:00:35.133
The curve shows coverage, also called depth, the number of reads over each base.

00:00:35.133 --> 00:00:37.343
Here it averages about 45 reads.

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:43.471
Type a 300 base range into the position field above the viewer and press Return.

00:00:43.471 --> 00:00:44.917
Each read is now a bar.

00:00:44.917 --> 00:00:47.810
Where it matches the reference it stays plain.

00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:49.822
A different base shows in color.

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:53.945
You carry two copies of each chromosome.

00:00:53.945 --> 00:01:00.449
At position 2,078 every read has A where the reference has G, so both copies carry it.

00:01:00.449 --> 00:01:02.416
This is called homozygous.

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:07.342
At 2,162 about half the reads have T instead of A.

00:01:07.342 --> 00:01:09.253
Only one copy carries it.

00:01:09.253 --> 00:01:11.393
This is called heterozygous.

00:01:11.393 --> 00:01:17.967
The Variants tab under the viewer lists the calls a program made at these same places.

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:22.857
To learn more, read the manual chapter Reading an Alignment.

00:01:22.857 --> 00:01:26.012
More videos are on the Videos page of the website.
